@import copy from '../../components/copy.tscl'
@import docs from '../../layouts/docs.tscl'

<docs [title]="'Vite plugin'" [path]="'/reference/vite-plugin'" [description]="'The clarusjs Vite plugin: its routes option, and what it does in dev, build and preview.'">
  <h1>Vite plugin</h1>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim>import clarusjs from 'clarusjs/vite'

export default defineConfig({ plugins: [clarusjs()] })</code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>
  <h2>routes</h2>
  <p><strong>Type:</strong> <code>Record&lt;string, 'static' | 'server' | 'client'&gt;</code> · <strong>Default:</strong> <code>{}</code> (every route is static)</p>
  <p>When each route renders. Keys are paths (<code>/about</code>) or folders (<code>/account/*</code>, which also matches <code>/account</code>); the most specific one wins. An unknown mode, or a pattern that doesn't start with <code>/</code>, stops the build. See <a href="/guide/rendering-modes">Rendering modes</a>.</p>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim>clarusjs({ routes: { '/account/*': 'server' } })</code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>
  <h2>adapter</h2>
  <p><strong>Type:</strong> <code>'netlify'</code> · <strong>Default:</strong> none</p>
  <p>Where server routes deploy. With <code>'netlify'</code>, <code>vite build</code> writes <code>.netlify/v1/functions/clarusjs-server.mjs</code> (Netlify's Frameworks API, read from the site's package directory: in a monorepo, set Netlify's package directory to the folder with <code>vite.config.ts</code>, and don't also set <code>base</code> to it): a function that calls <code>dist/server/index.mjs</code> for the server routes' paths, with static files served first. With no server routes, no function is written.</p>
  <h2>fragments</h2>
  <p><strong>Type:</strong> <code>boolean</code> · <strong>Default:</strong> <code>false</code></p>
  <p>Fragment navigation. Every static and server page also answers at <code>&lt;path&gt;/index.frag.html</code>: its <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> and the element marked <code>data-cl-swap</code> (or its <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>). <code>vite build</code> writes those files next to each static page; server routes serve them from the handler (and, with <code>adapter: 'netlify'</code>, from the function).</p>
  <h2>links</h2>
  <p><strong>Type:</strong> <code>{{ '{' }} pages?: 'error' | 'warn' | 'off'; anchors?: 'error' | 'warn' | 'off'; strict?: boolean {{ '}' }}</code> · <strong>Default:</strong> <code>{{ '{' }} pages: 'error', anchors: 'warn' {{ '}' }}</code></p>
  <p>Link checking after <code>vite build</code> has written every page. <code>pages</code>: links to a page or file that doesn't exist. <code>anchors</code>: links to a <code>#id</code> the target page doesn't have (pages rendered in the browser are skipped). Each problem names the source file and line, with the closest match suggested. <code>strict: true</code> makes warnings fail the build too.</p>
  <h2>head</h2>
  <p><strong>Type:</strong> <code>{{ '{' }} titleTemplate?: string; tags?: [tag, attrs, content?][] {{ '}' }}</code></p>
  <p>Every page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. <code>titleTemplate</code> (<code>'%s · Site'</code>) wraps the document's <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>. <code>tags</code> (<code>['meta', {{ '{' }} name: 'theme-color', content: '#fff' {{ '}' }}]</code>) go on every page, unless the page has its own tag with the same key: <code>title</code>, <code>meta</code> by <code>name</code>, <code>property</code>, <code>charset</code> or <code>http-equiv</code>, the canonical <code>link</code>, <code>base</code>.</p>
  <p>A page adds its own tags anywhere in its template with <code>&lt;cl-head&gt;…&lt;/cl-head&gt;</code>: they move into the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and win over the site's. A <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> there is used as written, without the template. For anything else, export <code>transformHead(head, page)</code> from <code>src/head.ts</code>: it gets the head's HTML and <code>{{ '{' }} route, path {{ '}' }}</code>, and returns the new HTML.</p>
  <p><code>jsonLd</code> (an object or a list) adds schema.org structured data to every page, one <code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;</code> each. In a template, a JSON-LD script takes one <code>{{ '{{' }} {{ '}}' }}</code> hole with the whole value, written as JSON (safe inside the script, never HTML-escaped): <code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;{{ '{{' }} {{ '{' }} '@type': 'Article', headline: title {{ '}' }} {{ '}}' }}&lt;/script&gt;</code>. Components with a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> can't have one: JSON-LD is rendered on the server.</p>
  <p>Analytics such as Google's gtag are site tags too. A script's content is written as given, and boolean attributes are <code>true</code>:</p>
  <pre verbatim><code verbatim>clarusjs({
  head: {
    tags: [
      ['script', { async: true, src: 'https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-XXXX' }, ''],
      ['script', {}, "window.dataLayer=window.dataLayer||[];function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments)}gtag('js',new Date());gtag('config','G-XXXX')"],
    ],
  },
})</code></pre>
  <p>Head scripts run once per full page load: with fragment navigation or client routes, later pages don't reload them. Analytics that count page views from history changes (gtag's enhanced measurement does) keep counting.</p>
  <h2>site</h2>
  <p><strong>Type:</strong> <code>{{ '{' }} url: string; name?: string; image?: string {{ '}' }}</code></p>
  <p>The site's public address (an absolute <code>http(s)</code> URL, or the config is rejected). Every page gets a canonical link and social cards: Open Graph <code>og:title</code> (the page title, without the template), <code>og:description</code> (its description meta), <code>og:url</code>, <code>og:type</code>, <code>og:site_name</code> (<code>name</code>), <code>og:image</code> and a Twitter card (<code>summary_large_image</code> with an image). They fill in like site tags: a page's own tags win, so an article can set <code>og:type</code> or its own <code>og:image</code> in <code>&lt;cl-head&gt;</code> (in Markdown, frontmatter <code>image</code>).</p>
  <p>Images are made absolute: <code>/og.png</code> is a public file under the base, <code>img/a.png</code> is relative to the page. Without <code>site</code>, a page with a relative <code>og:image</code> or <code>og:url</code> fails the build instead of shipping a card that silently breaks. Link checking also checks absolute links to the site itself.</p>
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>vite</strong>: renders <code>src/pages/**/*.tscl</code> on each request.</li>
    <li><strong>vite build</strong>: builds the client, then the server, then prerenders every static page to <code>dist/client</code>. Server routes are left to <code>dist/server/index.mjs</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>vite preview</strong>: serves the built pages at their routes, and server routes through the built handler.</li>
  </ul>
</docs>
